The key insight: to_lowercase() can change the byte length of characters. For example, the German ß uppercased then lowercased, or Turkish İ lowercased, produce different byte lengths. When we get byte offsets from `lower_text.find() or lower_text.match_indices()`, those offsets may not correspond to valid positions in the original text.

The correct approach is to build a mapping from lowercased byte positions back to original byte positions using `char_indices()`.

